You need three outcomes depending on today's energy level: "low" → rest day, "medium" → light session, anything else → full session.

```
if (energy === "low") {
  console.log("Rest day.");
} else if (energy === "medium") {
  console.log("Light session.");
} else {
  console.log("Full session.");
}
```

contentSections.body:
```
if (condition1) {
  // runs if condition1 is true
} else if (condition2) {
  // runs if condition1 is false AND condition2 is true
} else {
  // runs if none of the above matched
}
```

Each `else if` is only reached if every condition above it was false — which is why order matters. If you wrote `energy === "medium"` before `energy === "low"`, a low-energy day just checks the medium condition first, finds it false, and moves on; the chain still lands on the right branch eventually, but a chain with overlapping conditions (unlike this one) could quietly produce the wrong answer if the more specific case isn't checked first.

contentSections.scenario:
